  "readmeFile": "docs\\README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "First public release of Field Dependencies. Free to install and use, for personal and commercial work; closed\nsource. The terms are in LICENSE.txt.\n\n- Select a table and a field, choose the components to search, and run one analysis: forms, views, charts,\n  business rules, classic workflows, business process flows, reports, JavaScript web resources, plug-in steps\n  and images, plug-in and custom workflow activity code, custom actions, and the dependencies the platform\n  itself reports.\n- Every reference says how it uses the field (displayed, read, written, filter, trigger and more) and shows\n  its evidence. Compiled plug-ins and custom workflow activities are decompiled on your computer to show the\n  statement that touches the field. Nothing is sent to any other service.\n- Every reference is checked against the selected table: verified, context supported, uncertain table or\n  different table. Only verified references are counted; the others are listed separately.\n- Every reference states who built its implementation, whose component it is (the organization's,\n  Microsoft's, or unknown) and how it runs. Components Microsoft ships are hidden until *Show Microsoft /\n  platform references* is ticked, and every count says how many are hidden. A step the platform registers to\n  run a real-time workflow or a business rule is shown with that process; one whose process cannot be\n  identified stays listed.\n- Every analysis states its coverage: which search targets were searched completely, partially, not at all,\n  or failed.\n- Export to CSV, JSON, Excel or a self-contained HTML report. A standard export holds what the result pages\n  list, a full export everything that was found; every file states which of the two it is.\n- A workspace made for reading results and code: the result list and the evidence share the page, with a\n  divider you can drag (remembered between sessions); *Focus Code* gives the code most of the height and\n  *Restore Layout* brings your division back; the code always has the full width, and the technical details\n  have a page of their own. The field summary, the filters and the note at the bottom take a line each.\n\nKnown limitations: no static analysis can prove that a field is unused. The tool was developed and tested for\nDynamics 365 Customer Engagement 9.1 on-premises; Dataverse / Dynamics 365 online has not been tested. On a\nlarge organization an analysis that includes plug-in and custom workflow activity code can take many minutes.",
  "requireLicenseAcceptance": true,
  "summary": "Analyze field dependencies and references across Microsoft Dynamics 365 and Dataverse components.",
